          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Generally by a
            %em higher spin field theory
            is meant a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of spin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 2
            (recalling that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+field"} spinor field
            has spin 1/2, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            has spin 1, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
            field has spin 3/2 and the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            has spin 2).
          %p
            Folklore had it that all higher spin field theories with
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"}> mass
              \-less
            higher spin fields are inconsistent due to negative norm states (“ghost”) appearing in their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N} = 1
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/12-dimensional+supergravity"} 12-dimensional supergravity
            for more on this).
          %p
            But then it was discovered that there is a higher spin gauge theory (
            %a{:href => "#Vasiliev96"}> Vasiliev 96
            ) which is a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field content
            is an
            %em infinite tower
            of massless fields of ever higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
          %p
            Ever since the refine folklore says that higher spin theories with a
            %em finite
            number of higher spin field species is inconsistent, but that an infinite tower can fix the problem (…add reference…).
          %p
            One way that higher spin gauge theories are thought to naturally arise is as the limiting case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
            when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+tension"} string tension
            is sent to zero (
            %a{:href => "#HenneauxTeitelboim87"}> Henneaux-Teitelboim 87, section 2
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gross88"} Gross 88
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SagnottiTsulaia03"} Sagnotti-Tsulaia 03
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Bonelli03"} Bonelli 03
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            contains beyond the massless particles of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theory an infinite tower of massive excitations, of ever higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            %em tensionless limit
            – this is notably so for compactifications on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spaces"} anti de Sitter spaces
            of small radius. In this limit the string spectrum looks like an infinite collection of massless
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particles"} spinning particles
            for ever higher spin. Due to their common origin in the string, these share intricate relations among each other, which are argued to be described by higher spin gauge theory. (Notice that at least closed bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
            is itself already a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+tension"} string tension
            to zero, see at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "string+field+theory#AsAnInfinityCSTheory"} closed string field theory – As an ∞-Chern-Simons theory
          %p At present there are several classes of higher spin theories that evade the no-go theorems: (1) massless and conformal higher spin theories in three dimensions can be formulated as Chern-Simons theories; (2) conformal higher spin gravity in four dimensions is a higher spin extension of the Weyl gravity; (3) Chiral higher spin gravity is a higher spin extension of self-dual Yang-Mills theory and self-dual Gravity. At the same time the holographic higher spin theories face certain difficulties due non-locality implied by the AdS/CFT duality to vector models
